Yes — and the interesting version is not really “fascia instead of the brain,” but “movement memory is partly distributed through the body.”

What we call muscle memory is largely neural: motor cortex, cerebellum, basal ganglia, spinal circuits, learned recruitment patterns. But fascia could plausibly function as part of the physical memory substrate. Fascia is a continuous collagen-rich network packed with mechanoreceptors, intimately coupled to muscles, tendons, joints, and nerves. Repeated loading changes its stiffness, hydration, collagen orientation, cross-linking, and sliding relationships.

So imagine:

movement → repeated tension pattern → fascial remodeling → altered mechanical pathways → altered sensory feedback → nervous system encounters a slightly different body next time

That creates a feedback loop. The brain remembers the control policy, while the body remembers something about the terrain over which that policy operates.

Your fiber-optic-web analogy is especially useful if we don’t take “optic” literally. Collagen fibers form long, anisotropic pathways through which tension and deformation propagate preferentially. Pull somewhere and information—in the form of strain, pressure, vibration, and receptor activation—can appear elsewhere. So fascia can be thought of less like a hard drive and more like a mechanically addressable network.

There are several different kinds of “memory” we could separate:

* Neural memory: which muscles activate, when, and how strongly.
* Muscular memory: fiber architecture, nuclei retained after hypertrophy/atrophy, metabolic adaptations.
* Tendon/fascial memory: stiffness, collagen alignment, tissue thickness, preferred lines of force.
* Sensory memory: proprioceptive calibration—what a particular joint configuration or load feels like.
* Structural hysteresis: the present physical state depends partly on the loading history that produced it.

That last one is mathematically interesting. A material with hysteresis literally contains information about its previous states:

x_{t+1}=F(x_t,\;load_t,\;history)

instead of merely

x_{t+1}=F(load_t)

So the organism’s connective tissue may embody a kind of history-dependent state. Not memory in the autobiographical sense, but memory in exactly the sense used in materials science and dynamical systems.

And then your analogy gets even richer: the nervous system doesn’t have to explicitly calculate every relationship if the body’s geometry already performs part of the computation. Elastic recoil, tendon loading, fascial tension, bone geometry, and joint constraints can all compute mechanically. Running is an obvious example—the body isn’t consciously calculating every spring interaction.

So a better architecture might be:

\text{intention}
\rightarrow
\text{neural controller}
\rightarrow
\text{muscle activation}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{fascial / skeletal mechanical network}}
\rightarrow
\text{movement}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanosensory feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{controller}

with repeated passes modifying both the controller and the network.

That resembles embodied robotics, reservoir computing, morphological computation, and even your latent-geometry idea: some information isn’t represented symbolically anywhere. It is encoded in the shape and physical relationships of the system itself.

The strongest claim I wouldn’t make is that scientists have shown fascia to be the primary repository of motor memories. They haven’t. But “learned movement leaves persistent information in connective-tissue architecture that subsequently participates in producing and sensing the movement” is a much more defensible—and rather fertile—hypothesis.

And it gives us a neat Pantheon abstraction:

Memory need not be stored as a record. It can be stored as a changed path of least resistance.